---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Questions, answered · DuBose, M.D." active="questions">

  <!-- Intro -->
  <section class="page-title">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>Questions, answered</h1>
      <p class="lede">If your question isn&rsquo;t here, ask it in the free conversation.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="pillbar">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="row">
        <a href="#model">The model</a>
        <a href="#money">Money &amp; coverage</a>
        <a href="#availability">Availability</a>
        <a href="#medicine">The medicine</a>
        <a href="#starting">Starting &amp; stopping</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">

      <div class="faq-group" id="model">
        <h2>The model</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          <details>
            <summary>Is this health insurance?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              No. It is a direct agreement between you and me for my
              services as your doctor. Keep your insurance for everything
              else: labs, medicines, specialists, hospitals.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Is this &ldquo;concierge medicine&rdquo;?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              It shares one idea with concierge practices: a small panel
              and real access. But concierge fees typically run $2,400 to
              $5,000 a year on top of insurance billing. This is $100 a
              month, I bill no one, and there are no tiers. The formal
              name is direct primary care.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Why is it only $100 a month? What&rsquo;s the catch?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              The practice is small by design: no office building, no
              billing department, no staff, and a limited number of
              patients. The limits are stated, not hidden. I keep
              business hours. I am not an emergency service. I take real
              time away each year. When my panel is full, it is full.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>I already have doctors I like. Does this still make sense?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Often, yes. You can keep every doctor you have, including
              another primary care doctor. Many members use me as an
              added layer of support: to explain, to coordinate, and to
              follow up between their other visits.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What does &ldquo;unhurried&rdquo; actually mean?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Visits are scheduled for as long as they need. A first
              visit is up to 90 minutes, and no visit ends because a
              schedule says so. I keep my panel small enough that this
              stays true.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-group" id="money">
        <h2>Money &amp; coverage</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          <details>
            <summary>Besides $100 a month, what will I pay?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Nothing to me. Care from others is billed by them, usually
              through your insurance: labs, imaging, medicines, vaccines,
              specialists, urgent care, hospitals. Where I can get
              discounted lab pricing, I pass it through at my cost and
              tell you the number first.
              More: <a href="/pricing">Pricing &rarr;</a>
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Are there extra fees? Forms? Missed visits?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              No. Paperwork is included: disability and FMLA forms,
              letters, prior authorizations. There are no no-show fees.
              The only pass-throughs are procedure supplies at my cost,
              named before the procedure.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>I&rsquo;m on Medicare. Can I join?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Yes. Federal rules require one extra document, a Medicare
              private contract, because I have formally opted out of
              billing Medicare for my services. Your Medicare keeps
              paying for labs, imaging, specialists, prescriptions, and
              hospital care as before. More: <a href="/pricing">Pricing &rarr;</a>
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>I&rsquo;m on Medicaid. Can I join?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Yes, with one signed form required by state rules. If you
              have traditional fee-for-service Medicaid, we talk first
              about how tests and prescriptions I order are covered. One
              exception: federal rules bar private arrangements for
              certain combined Medicare-Medicaid programs (QMB). If that
              is your situation, I will help you find good care instead.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can I pay with my HSA?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Possibly. Recent federal law may allow HSA funds for direct
              primary care fees like this one. Confirm with your tax
              advisor. I will give you the documentation you need.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What if $100 a month is out of reach?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Tell me. I keep a limited number of reduced-rate
              memberships for people who could not otherwise afford care.
              It is a conversation, not an application.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-group" id="availability">
        <h2>Availability</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          <details>
            <summary>How fast do you respond to messages?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Within 1&ndash;2 business days, usually faster during business
              hours. If something needs attention sooner, say so in the
              message. Messaging is never the path for urgent problems or
              emergencies.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What about nights, weekends, and emergencies?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              I keep business hours, Monday through Friday. Emergencies
              like chest pain, stroke signs, severe injury, or thoughts
              of self-harm are always 911 and the ER. For urgent same-day
              problems when I&rsquo;m unavailable, use urgent care, then
              tell me. This is what keeps the attention real the rest of
              the time.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What happens when you are away?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              I take real time away each year. You will know in advance.
              I still check incoming results and messages for anything
              important. Refills are arranged before I go. The emergency
              and urgent-care pathways work exactly as always. Away means
              slower, never unaware.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Do you make house calls?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Yes. When an exam needs hands-on care and you are within my
              service area, I come to you, scheduled, at no extra charge.
              Most care happens by video, with clinic space when a
              procedure needs a proper room.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-group" id="medicine">
        <h2>The medicine</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          <details>
            <summary>What do you treat?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Full-scope adult primary care: blood pressure, diabetes,
              cholesterol, thyroid, everyday illness, prevention and
              screening, depression and anxiety, ADHD, weight management
              including modern medicines, and sleep problems.
              More: <a href="/help">What I can help with &rarr;</a>
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What is your training, exactly?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              M.D. from the Texas A&amp;M College of Medicine. Internal
              medicine internship at George Washington University. Years
              of part-time primary care alongside leading NIH-funded
              research on aging and health technology. Now pursuing
              lifestyle medicine certification. I did not complete a
              residency. The full picture: <a href="/about">About &rarr;</a>
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Do you prescribe controlled medications?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Within a careful written policy, yes, including ADHD
              medicines where appropriate. Federal and Texas telemedicine
              rules apply, some medicines require in-person evaluation or
              monitoring, and I prescribe conservatively. If your main
              goal is a specific controlled prescription, I am probably
              not the right fit.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What if I need a specialist or the hospital?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              You get the right specialist, a real referral, and a doctor
              who stays involved. In the hospital, the hospital&rsquo;s
              doctors lead. I stay informed and manage your care when you
              are home.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-group" id="starting">
        <h2>Starting &amp; stopping</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          <details>
            <summary>What is the free conversation like?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Thirty minutes by video. You tell me what is going on. I
              ask questions and tell you honestly if I can help. No forms
              beyond your name and email, no pressure, no bill.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How do I join, and how fast can we start?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              After we talk: sign the membership agreement, add a payment
              method, and we schedule your first full visit, usually
              within two weeks, after I have read your records.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Is there a contract or minimum commitment?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              No. Month to month from day one, no enrollment fee, cancel
              anytime effective at the end of your billing month. The
              complete terms: <a href="/agreement">the full membership
              agreement &rarr;</a>
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What happens to my records if I leave?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              They are yours. A complete electronic copy is always free
              and sent promptly to you and your next doctor. Active
              issues are handed off safely, and prescriptions are bridged
              up to 90 days where clinically safe.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can my spouse or family join?</summary>
            <p class="a">
              Any adult can join, each with their own membership at the
              same $100. A couple is $200 a month. I care for adults
              only, so children need a pediatrician.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Closing CTA -->
  <section class="section cta-band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2>Still have a question? Ask me directly.</h2>
        <a class="btn btn-inverse" href="/book">Book a free conversation</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
